Q: how to select the value from &quot;Span Type dropdown&quot; in Selenium webdriver A: <p>Please try the below code:</p>
<pre><code>driver.findElement(By.id(“dropdownField”)).sendKeys(“mention text of required 
 value from dropdown”); //send required option in dropdown field
driver.findElement(By.id(“option1”)).click();
</code></pre>
<p>Or try with the below code:</p>
<pre><code>List&lt;WebElement&gt; options = driver.findElements(By.xpath(“”));
for(WebElement option : options) {
if (option.getText().contains(“mention text of required value from dropdown”)) 
{
 option.click();
 break;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Please refer to the <a href="https://biswasarpita.medium.com/selecting-dropdown-option-with-selenium-19ef06e1d5fa" rel="nofollow noreferrer">link</a> for more details- how to select a particular value in a dropdown without using the methods of Select class in Selenium.</p>

Q: Storage duration of compound literals in C A: <blockquote>
<p>Storage duration of compound literals in C</p>
</blockquote>
<p>With the below code, <em>compound literal</em> is valid until the closing <code>}</code> after creation.  In this case that is where the function ends.  The <em>address</em> to that compound literal (saved in <code>foo-&gt;i</code>) ceases to be valid then too.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f(struct s *foo, const int a, const int b)
{
    foo-&gt;i = (int []){a, b};
} // Valid until here.
</code></pre>
<p>After that point, dereferencing that address is <em>undefined behavior</em> (UB), as in the calling code <code>foo.i[0]</code>.</p>
<p>Technically, even <em>reading</em> that address is also <em>undefined behavior</em> (UB), yet I have never come across a  system that did not tolerate the read of an invalid object address.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Since the code above runs without error, does it mean that the compilers created the array (int []){34, 35} in the global scope, therefore it has static storage duration?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;code above runs without error&quot; is an incorrect conclusion.  OP's observed results are reliant on UB, which may differ in the future.</p>
<p><code>(int []){34, 35}</code> in the global scope --&gt; no.<br />
... therefore it has static storage --&gt; duration? --&gt; no.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Should I stop using clang lol (jk)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No.</p>
<hr />
<p>Alternatives include:</p>
<p><strong>Allocate</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// foo-&gt;i = (int []){a, b};

#define CL (int []){a, b}
foo-&gt;i = malloc(sizeof (CL);
if (foo-&gt;i == NULL) {
  Handle_OOM_with TBD_code();
} else {
  memcpy(foo-&gt;i, &amp;CL, sizeof CL);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Make static</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f(struct s *foo, const int a, const int b)
{
    static int cl[] = {a, b};
    foo-&gt;i = &amp;cl;
}
</code></pre>

Q: limit the number of log files using log4j2 RollingFileAppender A: <p>You can</p>
<ul>
<li><p>create a thread in your Java program to manage old log files or create a process outside your Java program to do it.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>or use a trick with the naming policy for the</strong> <code>RollingFileAppender</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>It's working, and it avoids managing ton of old log files.</p>
<p>For example when you create your <code>RollingFileAppender</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>RollingFileAppender rollingFileAppender = RollingFileAppender
    .newBuilder()
    .setImmediateFlush(true)
    .setLayout(rollingFilelayout)
    // .withFilePattern(path + &quot;%i.log&quot;) usually &lt;-- you set an increasing naming 
    // policy for old log files
    .withFilePattern(path + &quot;.log.previous&quot;) // &lt;-- here for example you will have only 
    // ONE backup file
    .withFileName(path + &quot;.log&quot;)
    .setName(String.format(&quot;fileAppender-%s&quot;, loggerName))
    .withPolicy(SizeBasedTriggeringPolicy.createPolicy(&quot;10MB&quot;)) // &lt;-- dont forget to set 
    // the file size for the old log file according to your needs
    .build();
rollingFileAppender.start();
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>In a full use case :</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class LogUtil {

    private static final ConcurrentHashMap&lt;String, Logger&gt; loggersWithFile =
        new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();

    public static Logger getLoggerWithFile(String loggerName) {
        if (!LogUtil.loggersWithFile.containsKey(loggerName)) {
            Logger logger = getLogger(loggerName);
            LoggerContext loggerContext =
                (LoggerContext) LogManager.getContext(false);
            PatternLayout rollingFilelayout =
                PatternLayout.newBuilder().withPattern(
                    // Constants.ZONE_ID = &quot;Europe/Paris&quot;
                    &quot;%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}{&quot; + Constants.ZONE_ID
                        + &quot;}-%-5level- &quot; + &quot;%m%n&quot;).build();
            // I put log files in a custom log folder
            File file = new File(String.format(&quot;log/%s&quot;, loggerName));
            if (!file.getParentFile().exists()) {
                boolean isLogDirectoryCreated = file.getParentFile().mkdirs();
                if (!isLogDirectoryCreated) {
                    throw new RuntimeException(&quot;Log directory cannot be created&quot;);
                }
            }
            String path = file.getPath();
            RollingFileAppender rollingFileAppender = RollingFileAppender
                .newBuilder()
                .setImmediateFlush(true)
                .setLayout(rollingFilelayout)
                .withFilePattern(path + &quot;.log.previous&quot;)
                .withFileName(path + &quot;.log&quot;)
                .setName(String.format(&quot;fileAppender-%s&quot;, loggerName))
                .withPolicy(SizeBasedTriggeringPolicy.createPolicy(&quot;1KB&quot;))
                .build();
            rollingFileAppender.start();
            loggerContext
                .getLogger(loggerName)
                .addAppender(rollingFileAppender);
            loggerContext.updateLoggers();
            LogUtil.loggersWithFile.put(loggerName, logger);
            return logger;
        }
        else {
            return LogUtil.loggersWithFile.get(loggerName);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then you do where you need a logger</p>
<pre><code>public class MyClassWhichNeedsALogger {

    private static final Logger LOGGER = LogUtil.getLoggerWithFile(&quot;myClass&quot;);
</code></pre>
